Thank you.

00:04:32.770 --> 00:04:38.110
So as we dive in,
so quick survey.

00:04:38.110 --> 00:04:41.500
So when it comes to IoT
and in the audience here,

00:04:41.500 --> 00:04:44.440
just a quick raise of hands
in terms of how many folks

00:04:44.440 --> 00:04:49.010
are experimenting with
IoT in their organization,

00:04:49.010 --> 00:04:50.980
which is like
somebody, somewhere

00:04:50.980 --> 00:04:55.030
has a Raspberry Pi doing
something, or an Arduino,

00:04:55.030 --> 00:04:57.630
or an Android Things connecting?

00:04:57.630 --> 00:04:58.360
Great.

00:04:58.360 --> 00:05:01.240
It looks like the vast majority.

00:05:01.240 --> 00:05:02.540
We see that as well.

00:05:02.540 --> 00:05:05.650
And there's ages of IoT.

00:05:05.650 --> 00:05:07.660
We're still very much
at the dawn of IoT.

00:05:07.660 --> 00:05:09.700
So I think we're all
together in this journey

00:05:09.700 --> 00:05:11.830
to figure out how IoT functions.

00:05:11.830 --> 00:05:15.880
But we do see a lot of folks
who want to experience with IoT

00:05:15.880 --> 00:05:17.440
have connected something.

00:05:17.440 --> 00:05:19.780
So it's like the
connectivity age maybe

00:05:19.780 --> 00:05:22.370
we're in the middle of or maybe
at the end of in terms of,

00:05:22.370 --> 00:05:24.590
can I just get a
device connected?

00:05:24.590 --> 00:05:29.470
So, the next question is around
how many folks in the audience

00:05:29.470 --> 00:05:35.140
have full-blown IoT products
supported in the field

00:05:35.140 --> 00:05:37.930
with customers using
it, your call center

00:05:37.930 --> 00:05:41.560
can answer questions, your
documentation warranty

00:05:41.560 --> 00:05:45.510
is out there, how many
of the folks have that?

00:05:45.510 --> 00:05:48.090
Less, which is what we expect.

00:05:48.090 --> 00:05:51.490
So that's a lot of
the things that we

00:05:51.490 --> 00:05:53.610
want to talk about
today is that gap.

00:05:53.610 --> 00:05:54.880
IoT is new.

00:05:54.880 --> 00:05:58.240
So we don't expect a
lot of IoT products

00:05:58.240 --> 00:06:00.460
that are fully out
there in the field.

00:06:00.460 --> 00:06:04.570
But there is a
pretty dramatic gap

00:06:04.570 --> 00:06:09.850
that sitting in between
the amount of projects

00:06:09.850 --> 00:06:12.700
being piloted and the amount
of projects in the field.

00:06:12.700 --> 00:06:14.740
So there's a term that's
being thrown about.

00:06:14.740 --> 00:06:18.880
I look at it with whimsy,
which is "pilot purgatory."

00:06:18.880 --> 00:06:20.110
if you Google it--

00:06:20.110 --> 00:06:21.140
it's a generic term.

00:06:21.140 --> 00:06:23.544
But it's very much being
associated with IoT.

00:06:23.544 --> 00:06:27.340
And what it means is
we started a project,

00:06:27.340 --> 00:06:29.410
we haven't finished it.

00:06:29.410 --> 00:06:33.790
We're trying to figure out how
does the connectivity work?

00:06:33.790 --> 00:06:35.770
How do all these
components come together?

00:06:35.770 --> 00:06:39.280
And how does it exactly
provide business value

00:06:39.280 --> 00:06:44.600
for my boss, my shareholders, my
university, whatever it may be?

00:06:44.600 --> 00:06:46.540
So this concept of
pilot purgatory I think

00:06:46.540 --> 00:06:48.580
is very relevant
when it comes to ROI.

00:06:51.340 --> 00:06:53.830
One thing that
we're seeing is what

00:06:53.830 --> 00:06:58.750
is one of the major
challenges around why

00:06:58.750 --> 00:07:02.810
products and projects get
stuck in pilot purgatory?

00:07:02.810 --> 00:07:04.750
So this is just a
study by McKinsey.

00:07:04.750 --> 00:07:07.640
There's many such
studies that are similar.

00:07:07.640 --> 00:07:09.610
But this is a good
one to take it

00:07:09.610 --> 00:07:13.370
as an example, which is around
84% of the companies that

00:07:13.370 --> 00:07:16.120
started a pilot are
still in it a year later.

00:07:16.120 --> 00:07:17.650
That's a lot.

00:07:17.650 --> 00:07:18.610
And IoT is new.

00:07:18.610 --> 00:07:20.290
And the promise of IoT is great.

00:07:20.290 --> 00:07:25.750
So our bosses allow us some
flexibility to run pilots.

00:07:25.750 --> 00:07:31.000
But a year's a long time 30% are
still in a pilot two years in.

00:07:31.000 --> 00:07:37.400
And around 30% of projects
have gone to production.

00:07:37.400 --> 00:07:40.180
So 70% of the projects
haven't even exited pilot.

00:07:40.180 --> 00:07:42.670
So there's something here
holding projects back.

00:07:42.670 --> 00:07:45.460
According to this study,
the top three factors

00:07:45.460 --> 00:07:49.570
are executive support, so
whatever keeps the CEO up

00:07:49.570 --> 00:07:51.550
at night, is IoT solving it?

00:07:51.550 --> 00:07:55.240
Maybe not, strong
business case and also

00:07:55.240 --> 00:07:57.499
strong internal
organizational alignment.

00:07:57.499 --> 00:07:59.290
So I think what's
interesting here is these

00:07:59.290 --> 00:08:00.860
are not technical challenges.

00:08:00.860 --> 00:08:02.600
These are business challenges.

00:08:02.600 --> 00:08:05.620
So the thesis is that
many of the reasons we're

00:08:05.620 --> 00:08:07.930
stuck in pilot
purgatory isn't just

00:08:07.930 --> 00:08:10.010
all the technology is
getting woven together.

00:08:10.010 --> 00:08:12.500
It's how do we establish ROI?

00:08:12.500 --> 00:08:17.620
And what's a methodology to
find the revenue inside of IoT?

00:08:17.620 --> 00:08:19.840
So, why and how?

00:08:19.840 --> 00:08:23.830
Certainly the how of IoT is
super interesting, certainly

00:08:23.830 --> 00:08:25.187
for geeks like me.

00:08:25.187 --> 00:08:26.770
Like connecting all
this stuff, here's

00:08:26.770 --> 00:08:30.760
an example of some underwater
technology, underwater cameras

00:08:30.760 --> 00:08:33.789
that Google has that we
partner up with [? Katelyn. ?]

00:08:33.789 --> 00:08:37.674
And that's what's powering
underwater Street View.

00:08:37.674 --> 00:08:39.340
So if you google
Underwater Street View,

00:08:39.340 --> 00:08:43.480
you can explore the Great
Barrier Reef, very cool.

00:08:43.480 --> 00:08:45.430
If you're building
a device like this,

00:08:45.430 --> 00:08:48.250
then I recommend we
spend a lot of time

00:08:48.250 --> 00:08:50.210
technically proving this.

00:08:50.210 --> 00:08:51.910
How does a device
work underwater?

00:08:51.910 --> 00:08:53.920
How does Wi-Fi work underwater?

00:08:53.920 --> 00:08:56.550
It sounds like a Google
interview question now

00:08:56.550 --> 00:08:57.740
that I think of it.

00:08:57.740 --> 00:08:59.240
I have I actually have no idea.

00:08:59.240 --> 00:09:02.020
So, if this is what
you're dealing with,

00:09:02.020 --> 00:09:05.625
then spend a lot of time
with technology inside

00:09:05.625 --> 00:09:06.250
of your pilots.

00:09:06.250 --> 00:09:09.520
If it's not, then the
suggestion is that we

00:09:09.520 --> 00:09:10.630
should trust our devices.

00:09:10.630 --> 00:09:12.250
We will get them connected.

00:09:12.250 --> 00:09:14.640
Very rarely do we see
a device that can't be.

00:09:14.640 --> 00:09:17.560
The challenge is there's a lot
of IoT technology out there.

00:09:17.560 --> 00:09:21.040
So how do I get the
right security protocols,

00:09:21.040 --> 00:09:22.420
the right conductivity
protocols,

00:09:22.420 --> 00:09:25.450
keep it secure, create
a device registry?

00:09:25.450 --> 00:09:27.132
Basically, it's an
assembly challenge

00:09:27.132 --> 00:09:28.840
of getting the right
components assembled

00:09:28.840 --> 00:09:30.640
to create an IoT solution.

00:09:30.640 --> 00:09:32.680
But we should all have
faith, for the most

00:09:32.680 --> 00:09:35.750
part, that the device
will be connected.

00:09:35.750 --> 00:09:39.400
But the challenge
is really the why.

00:09:39.400 --> 00:09:42.100
If we spend a lot of our
time, 80% of our effort,

00:09:42.100 --> 00:09:45.250
on the how, and that's
what our labs look

00:09:45.250 --> 00:09:49.330
like, and maybe 20% on the why,
we're stuck in pilot purgatory.

00:09:49.330 --> 00:09:51.500
We need to balance
that out a bit.

00:09:51.500 --> 00:09:54.490
So what we've done is worked
with a number of customers

00:09:54.490 --> 00:09:59.710
to come up with a framework
that is pretty simple where

00:09:59.710 --> 00:10:03.280
we can discover the why.

00:10:03.280 --> 00:10:04.440
Where is the ROI?

00:10:04.440 --> 00:10:06.670
And that's what I want
to present right now.

00:10:06.670 --> 00:10:09.850
So this is one of the
key slides that we

00:10:09.850 --> 00:10:12.100
show some customers
and prospects

00:10:12.100 --> 00:10:13.480
and walk them through.

00:10:13.480 --> 00:10:15.820
So there's direct ROI.

00:10:15.820 --> 00:10:17.230
It's the best type.

00:10:17.230 --> 00:10:20.920
So I did this IoT project,
and because of it, I

00:10:20.920 --> 00:10:23.560
derived actual value.

00:10:23.560 --> 00:10:25.870
So when it comes to
direct ROI, there's

00:10:25.870 --> 00:10:27.727
a couple of sides in
the balance sheet.

00:10:27.727 --> 00:10:28.310
Simple, right?

00:10:28.310 --> 00:10:29.050
I can sell more.

00:10:29.050 --> 00:10:30.190
I can save.

00:10:30.190 --> 00:10:32.140
One way to sell more
is feature enablement.

00:10:32.140 --> 00:10:33.580
It's a cooler product.

00:10:33.580 --> 00:10:36.880
So if you think of
B2C, or even B2B,

00:10:36.880 --> 00:10:39.430
if I have a product
that has IoT capability,

00:10:39.430 --> 00:10:40.840
it has more features.

00:10:40.840 --> 00:10:42.700
Because of that,
people may buy it.

00:10:42.700 --> 00:10:44.740
It's also a great
reason to refresh,

00:10:44.740 --> 00:10:48.490
so upgrade to the IoT
product versus the one that

00:10:48.490 --> 00:10:50.810
wasn't, which is great.

00:10:50.810 --> 00:10:52.400
All revenue is good revenue.

00:10:52.400 --> 00:10:55.480
The problem here is it's
a one-time purchase.

00:10:55.480 --> 00:10:59.260
And every time we IoT enable the
device, the cost of that device

00:10:59.260 --> 00:11:03.430
goes up because we have to pay
to keep that device connected.

00:11:03.430 --> 00:11:07.360
What we've seen is that device
can cost anywhere from $0.25

00:11:07.360 --> 00:11:11.350
a year to $1 a more a
year to get connected.

00:11:11.350 --> 00:11:13.600
That increases your
bill of materials.

00:11:13.600 --> 00:11:16.810
And if your margins are
high on your products,

00:11:16.810 --> 00:11:19.960
and your lifespan is relatively
low on your products, maybe

00:11:19.960 --> 00:11:23.530
five to 10 years, you may
be able to absorb the bill

00:11:23.530 --> 00:11:25.000
of materials difference.

00:11:25.000 --> 00:11:28.180
But very few companies
that we deal with can.

00:11:28.180 --> 00:11:29.750
It's a competitive market.

00:11:29.750 --> 00:11:31.900
So what other
techniques do we have

00:11:31.900 --> 00:11:34.819
to generate revenue beyond
just the individual sales?

00:11:34.819 --> 00:11:36.985
Some of these other categories,
so sales enablement.

00:11:36.985 --> 00:11:39.700
And what we're talking about
here is replenishables.

00:11:39.700 --> 00:11:41.860
It's the razor and blades story.

00:11:41.860 --> 00:11:45.580
So I'll give you the razor for
free if you'll buy my blades.

00:11:45.580 --> 00:11:47.230
So from a replenishable
perspective,

00:11:47.230 --> 00:11:50.440
if we can create a product where
we can identify that something

00:11:50.440 --> 00:11:52.570
has been consumed,
create a subscription,

00:11:52.570 --> 00:11:54.880
automatically send
it to you, we can

00:11:54.880 --> 00:11:56.380
create a subscription model.

00:11:56.380 --> 00:11:59.724
And that's the nirvana of ROI.

00:11:59.724 --> 00:12:01.390
The next one down is
service enablement.

00:12:01.390 --> 00:12:05.710
And here you think of like
anything as a service.

00:12:05.710 --> 00:12:08.170
If you want to be cool
at an IoT cocktail party

00:12:08.170 --> 00:12:10.880
say, something as a service.

00:12:10.880 --> 00:12:13.330
So the idea is I am
an HVAC provider.

00:12:13.330 --> 00:12:17.650
But since I know the air
quality of the indoor building,

00:12:17.650 --> 00:12:20.320
I'll sell you air
quality as a service.

00:12:20.320 --> 00:12:22.930
And the better quality you
want, the more you'll pay me.

00:12:22.930 --> 00:12:25.210
And then I'll figure
out when to switch out

00:12:25.210 --> 00:12:26.500
all the various filters.

00:12:26.500 --> 00:12:28.180
So that's service enablement.

00:12:28.180 --> 00:12:29.810
And then the next is
scale and pricing,

00:12:29.810 --> 00:12:32.620
which is that if you become
operationally efficient enough,

00:12:32.620 --> 00:12:35.930
then sometimes you can
actually produce more product

00:12:35.930 --> 00:12:39.440
than you used to be able to and
maybe even charge a premium.

00:12:39.440 --> 00:12:42.490
So it's just another
avenue to achieve money.

00:12:42.490 --> 00:12:45.560
On the right-hand side
are around efficiencies.

00:12:45.560 --> 00:12:46.850
Think of a warehouse.

00:12:46.850 --> 00:12:49.430
So increase up time,
I added one more nine

00:12:49.430 --> 00:12:52.830
to my number of nines on
my manufacturing floor.

00:12:52.830 --> 00:12:55.190
Or you lowered risk
for your employees

00:12:55.190 --> 00:12:58.560
by IoT-enabling equipment
and make sure nothing hits

00:12:58.560 --> 00:12:59.810
somebody.

00:12:59.810 --> 00:13:02.060
Resource efficiency,
certainly, to make sure

00:13:02.060 --> 00:13:05.030
that you're consuming your
resources effectively and then

00:13:05.030 --> 00:13:06.500
have a higher quality product.

00:13:06.500 --> 00:13:09.290
So you may think a more
based on your vertical.

00:13:09.290 --> 00:13:12.470
But these ones we've
seen work again and again

00:13:12.470 --> 00:13:17.250
for 80% of the businesses we
work with, so direct revenue.

00:13:17.250 --> 00:13:19.880
Now we see all this
indirect revenue.

00:13:19.880 --> 00:13:22.010
So this is a
blessing and a curse.

00:13:22.010 --> 00:13:25.690
This indirect revenue
is the promise of IoT.

00:13:25.690 --> 00:13:29.620
It's where phrases like "data
is the new oil" comes from,

00:13:29.620 --> 00:13:32.630
or big data, and data science.

00:13:32.630 --> 00:13:34.430
And the idea is you collect--

00:13:34.430 --> 00:13:37.130
you sensor all your
data, put sensors on it,

00:13:37.130 --> 00:13:40.790
collect lots of data, and then
money comes out the other side,

00:13:40.790 --> 00:13:43.290
except very rarely
does that happen.

00:13:43.290 --> 00:13:47.380
These indirect revenue
impacts are meaningful.

00:13:47.380 --> 00:13:50.660
But if all of our business
cases are based on them,

00:13:50.660 --> 00:13:53.450
then we can definitely get
stuck in pilot purgatory

00:13:53.450 --> 00:13:56.540
because we're looking
for long-term money that

00:13:56.540 --> 00:13:57.920
is a little hard to connect.

00:13:57.920 --> 00:14:00.980
So to go around the wheel really
quickly in the upper right,

00:14:00.980 --> 00:14:03.940
targeted marketing.

00:14:03.940 --> 00:14:05.960
The idea is that
a manufacturer who

00:14:05.960 --> 00:14:07.850
makes whatever, a
dishwasher, now I

00:14:07.850 --> 00:14:10.142
get to know my customer better
through IoT, and because

00:14:10.142 --> 00:14:11.600
of that, because
I know my customer

00:14:11.600 --> 00:14:13.340
and my customer
segment, I can market

00:14:13.340 --> 00:14:14.436
to them more effectively.

00:14:14.436 --> 00:14:16.310
Building better products,
I see how customers

00:14:16.310 --> 00:14:17.780
are using my products.

00:14:17.780 --> 00:14:22.800
Therefore, my next rev is going
to be of a higher quality.

00:14:22.800 --> 00:14:25.670
And enhanced brand, I guess the
marketers could debate this.

00:14:25.670 --> 00:14:30.110
But in B2C anyway,
when I put IoT-enabled

00:14:30.110 --> 00:14:31.700
the brand becomes
a little cooler.

00:14:31.700 --> 00:14:36.710
A little blue light shows up,
and it's you're a cooler brand.

00:14:36.710 --> 00:14:37.930
New business services.

00:14:37.930 --> 00:14:40.960
So we've seen customers who
are in the electrical business.

00:14:40.960 --> 00:14:44.240
They'll make a surge suppressor.

00:14:44.240 --> 00:14:47.480
And now what they can see
is there's been a surge,

00:14:47.480 --> 00:14:49.230
maybe some of the
equipment is out.

00:14:49.230 --> 00:14:52.730
So what they can do is
join the gig economy

00:14:52.730 --> 00:14:56.570
and have electricians
who are roaming around

00:14:56.570 --> 00:14:59.970
give the deal to them
to go and fix the job.

00:14:59.970 --> 00:15:01.790
So I used to be a
manufacturer, and now I'm

00:15:01.790 --> 00:15:04.880
participating in the gig
economy, a brand new business

00:15:04.880 --> 00:15:05.660
opportunity.

00:15:05.660 --> 00:15:07.490
And then customer
engagement, this

00:15:07.490 --> 00:15:09.860
is where a customer calls
in your call center.

00:15:09.860 --> 00:15:12.140
Now you can see their device.

00:15:12.140 --> 00:15:14.810
Most manufacturers who
are participating in IoT

00:15:14.810 --> 00:15:17.330
know the customer basically
through a warranty card.

00:15:17.330 --> 00:15:20.780
But now through IoT in an app, I
can know the customer directly.

00:15:20.780 --> 00:15:21.994
That's a responsibility.

00:15:21.994 --> 00:15:23.660
But it's also an
opportunity to increase

00:15:23.660 --> 00:15:26.510
my NPS, my net promoter
scores because I

00:15:26.510 --> 00:15:27.650
can service the customer.

00:15:27.650 --> 00:15:29.420
I can get a first time fix.

00:15:29.420 --> 00:15:31.800
So all these are great.

00:15:31.800 --> 00:15:34.700
What we see as we go around the
horn here, work with customers,

00:15:34.700 --> 00:15:37.310
and say, check off as
many of these as you can,

00:15:37.310 --> 00:15:40.670
to say how you're
going to generate ROI.

00:15:40.670 --> 00:15:43.980
And what we'd love to
see is in early pilots--

00:15:43.980 --> 00:15:45.290
so many of us are in pilots.

00:15:45.290 --> 00:15:48.515
We just like a raise of hands.

00:15:48.515 --> 00:15:50.390
Try to get as much direct
revenue as possible

00:15:50.390 --> 00:15:53.120
because you can prove to
your business in a short term

00:15:53.120 --> 00:15:54.290
that this is valuable.

00:15:54.290 --> 00:15:56.780
And then around the outside,
the indirect revenue, maybe

00:15:56.780 --> 00:15:58.760
even larger opportunities.

00:15:58.760 --> 00:16:02.000
But those will come second
once you have more time,

00:16:02.000 --> 00:16:04.490
rolled out a larger pilot,
collected more data,

00:16:04.490 --> 00:16:06.590
and had the opportunity
to mine that data.

00:16:06.590 --> 00:16:08.282
The more checkmarks
the better, it's

00:16:08.282 --> 00:16:09.740
just a little bit
of a warning sign

00:16:09.740 --> 00:16:11.990
if they're all on the outside.

00:16:11.990 --> 00:16:16.730
So what I want to do now is
pull in Doug and pull in Phil

00:16:16.730 --> 00:16:20.780
and have them walk you through
the specific agriculture

00:16:20.780 --> 00:16:21.910
example.

00:16:21.910 --> 00:16:22.994
What do they find on this?

00:16:22.994 --> 00:16:24.368
What were the bets
that they were

00:16:24.368 --> 00:16:26.210
placing at the beginning
of their project?

00:16:26.210 --> 00:16:28.460
What was the reality
of what they found?

00:16:28.460 --> 00:16:32.000
And how can we all learn
from their mistakes--

00:16:32.000 --> 00:16:33.580
that's what we want to do--

00:16:33.580 --> 00:16:37.910
as well as what they
accomplished and then

00:16:37.910 --> 00:16:40.850
apply that, obviously,
to the folks in the room.

00:16:40.850 --> 00:16:43.230
So Doug, Phil.

00:16:43.230 --> 00:16:44.990
DOUG STANDLEY: Thank you, Adam.

