00:24:03.655 --> 00:24:05.590
a senior partner
there, a vice chairman,

00:24:05.590 --> 00:24:08.500
ended up scratching dirt.

00:24:08.500 --> 00:24:10.510
For my 50th birthday,
my wife decided

00:24:10.510 --> 00:24:11.750
to take me down to Argentina.

00:24:11.750 --> 00:24:13.150
We had a hell of a lot of fun.

00:24:13.150 --> 00:24:15.460
In fact, so much fun that
instead of buying a shirt,

00:24:15.460 --> 00:24:17.020
we bought a vineyard.

00:24:17.020 --> 00:24:19.930
And came home, 27-page
document in Spanish.

00:24:19.930 --> 00:24:21.349
Sure enough, I
had it translated.

00:24:21.349 --> 00:24:22.390
We had bought a vineyard.

00:24:22.390 --> 00:24:25.960
It's a condominium vineyard,
but a vineyard just the same.

00:24:25.960 --> 00:24:28.420
Around the same time, we
were moving out to Arizona

00:24:28.420 --> 00:24:29.230
from Connecticut.

00:24:29.230 --> 00:24:31.120
And while we were out
there driving around,

00:24:31.120 --> 00:24:33.370
I happened to comment to
her how much it reminded me

00:24:33.370 --> 00:24:35.620
of Mendoza, Argentina.

00:24:35.620 --> 00:24:37.957
So she decided to go out
and check around if there

00:24:37.957 --> 00:24:39.040
was a wine industry there.

00:24:39.040 --> 00:24:40.510
And sure enough, there was.

00:24:40.510 --> 00:24:43.220
And about six months later,
I got a text that said, hey,

00:24:43.220 --> 00:24:45.950
I bought 20 acres of land
down in Wilcox, Arizona.

00:24:45.950 --> 00:24:47.440
We're going to plant a vineyard.

00:24:47.440 --> 00:24:49.420
That's literally how
we got here today.

00:24:49.420 --> 00:24:52.060
At that point in time, to put--

00:24:52.060 --> 00:24:54.490
certainly my wife, who's
done a fabulous job with this

00:24:54.490 --> 00:24:55.820
into perspective--

00:24:55.820 --> 00:24:58.690
we have no live plants
in our house at all.

00:24:58.690 --> 00:25:00.092
They're all dead.

00:25:00.092 --> 00:25:02.050
So we had to replace them
all with silk plants.

00:25:02.050 --> 00:25:04.330
And so I had to ask her,
I said, what in God's name

00:25:04.330 --> 00:25:06.910
makes you think
you can grow vines?

00:25:06.910 --> 00:25:11.350
Suffice it to say, that was the
battle cry of challenge to her.

00:25:11.350 --> 00:25:13.450
And she has done
a magnificent job.

00:25:13.450 --> 00:25:16.500
And it has really
done wonders over--

00:25:16.500 --> 00:25:19.840
planted it in 2011,
so through today,

00:25:19.840 --> 00:25:21.790
it has made tremendous strides.

00:25:21.790 --> 00:25:23.590
And this is a
culmination of what

00:25:23.590 --> 00:25:27.820
we wanted to do to really make
sure we were growing the best

00:25:27.820 --> 00:25:30.310
grapes possible because
in Arizona, nobody

00:25:30.310 --> 00:25:31.630
knows the answer to that yet.

00:25:31.630 --> 00:25:34.570
It's very much like
our name, Deep Sky.

00:25:34.570 --> 00:25:35.770
It's exploration.

00:25:35.770 --> 00:25:37.090
There is no right or wrong.

00:25:37.090 --> 00:25:38.890
Nobody has the magic formula.

00:25:38.890 --> 00:25:41.555
Nio is helping us
find those formulas.

00:25:41.555 --> 00:25:42.930
DOUG STANDLEY: So
what I'll do is

00:25:42.930 --> 00:25:44.888
I'm going to just drop
you through a little bit

00:25:44.888 --> 00:25:47.630
of the user experience and then
frankly turn it over to Phil.

00:25:47.630 --> 00:25:49.130
And he'll tell me
where to navigate.

00:25:49.130 --> 00:25:50.790
And he'll take you
through how he uses

00:25:50.790 --> 00:25:54.030
the platform on a daily basis.

00:25:54.030 --> 00:25:57.870
So we start obviously with
the proverbial dashboard view,

00:25:57.870 --> 00:25:59.790
which he takes a glance at.

00:25:59.790 --> 00:26:02.250
He peeks at his irrigation.

00:26:02.250 --> 00:26:04.250
We have multiple ways of
capturing the weather--

00:26:04.250 --> 00:26:06.950
macro and all the way
down to micro, even down

00:26:06.950 --> 00:26:08.530
to leaf moisture.

00:26:08.530 --> 00:26:11.300
The interesting thing about
farming, your macro forecast,

00:26:11.300 --> 00:26:13.760
your weather men can tell
you it's raining like hell,

00:26:13.760 --> 00:26:16.070
and you don't get an
iota of water in a farm.

00:26:16.070 --> 00:26:19.520
Or it could rain in one block of
a vineyard and not in another.

00:26:19.520 --> 00:26:21.290
But more importantly,
if it's windy,

00:26:21.290 --> 00:26:23.030
you won't absorb
any of that wine.

00:26:23.030 --> 00:26:25.220
So we have multiple
sensory capability

00:26:25.220 --> 00:26:27.710
down to even a weather
station on site

00:26:27.710 --> 00:26:29.675
as well as within the rows.

00:26:29.675 --> 00:26:32.250
PHIL ASMUNDSON: And just to
reemphasize, this is live.

00:26:32.250 --> 00:26:34.240
So it is 101 degrees
down there right now.

00:26:34.240 --> 00:26:37.550
DOUG STANDLEY: Right now.

00:26:37.550 --> 00:26:41.180
He takes a look at the
equipment diagnostics on pumps.

00:26:41.180 --> 00:26:43.460
Nio is fully instrumented
throughout that factory,

00:26:43.460 --> 00:26:45.820
so well all the way
through a pressure

00:26:45.820 --> 00:26:48.886
and flow throughout
various sensors.

00:26:48.886 --> 00:26:51.260
He has the ability to add
observations from the vineyard.

00:26:51.260 --> 00:26:53.900
This is something I'll
expound on a little bit later.

00:26:53.900 --> 00:26:55.534
He looks at the events
of the vineyard.

00:26:55.534 --> 00:26:57.700
And lastly, I'll spend a
little bit of time on this.

00:26:57.700 --> 00:26:58.790
It's certainly squint print.

00:26:58.790 --> 00:26:59.956
You can see it for yourself.

00:26:59.956 --> 00:27:02.202
But system view,
these are streaming

00:27:02.202 --> 00:27:04.160
perspective of what's
happening in the vineyard

00:27:04.160 --> 00:27:06.110
all the time by block.

00:27:06.110 --> 00:27:08.360
It's really a
powerful diagnostic.

00:27:08.360 --> 00:27:11.690
One thing that I learned
many years ago, fakery

00:27:11.690 --> 00:27:13.670
will only get you so
far in innovations.

00:27:13.670 --> 00:27:16.280
So everything you're
looking at here

00:27:16.280 --> 00:27:18.940
is real, and fortunately
for us, that the Asmundsons

00:27:18.940 --> 00:27:23.980
allow us to expose it to the
world, frankly real time.

00:27:23.980 --> 00:27:26.130
Phil, so let's walk
us through a day.

00:27:26.130 --> 00:27:27.230
PHIL ASMUNDSON: OK.

00:27:27.230 --> 00:27:32.492
For me, the dashboard-- go
down to weather real quickly.

00:27:32.492 --> 00:27:33.950
There's a couple
other things here,

00:27:33.950 --> 00:27:40.100
daily ET, that down here and
up there on the right side.

00:27:40.100 --> 00:27:41.990
No, the two inches,
you'll see daily ET.

00:27:41.990 --> 00:27:44.900
That's really a measure of
how much water the plants

00:27:44.900 --> 00:27:47.360
are basically expiring
to cool themselves down.

00:27:47.360 --> 00:27:49.130
That's what they use water for.

00:27:49.130 --> 00:27:50.480
It brings nutrients up.

00:27:50.480 --> 00:27:53.360
But they're also using
it to cool it down.

00:27:53.360 --> 00:28:01.340
So looking at those
forecasts, GDD is degree days.

00:28:01.340 --> 00:28:03.877
You need a certain number
of growing degree days

00:28:03.877 --> 00:28:05.210
in order to get your fruit ripe.

00:28:05.210 --> 00:28:07.250
And so we actually
track that so that we

00:28:07.250 --> 00:28:09.830
know about how far we might be
from harvest because we have

00:28:09.830 --> 00:28:11.970
that history from year to year.

00:28:11.970 --> 00:28:15.110
So there's more there than
just might meet the eye.

00:28:15.110 --> 00:28:16.710
That UV index is very important.

00:28:16.710 --> 00:28:18.710
That UV index is an
indicator of how much warmer

00:28:18.710 --> 00:28:20.967
that fruit will be
if it's in the sun,

00:28:20.967 --> 00:28:22.550
if it's in the direct
rays of the sun.

00:28:22.550 --> 00:28:24.740
You don't want that fruit
to warm up too much.

00:28:24.740 --> 00:28:27.014
Fortunately, we have a
good canopy to shield that.

00:28:27.014 --> 00:28:28.430
But let's go on
to the irrigation.

00:28:28.430 --> 00:28:30.350
This is where the
real stuff starts.

00:28:30.350 --> 00:28:38.390
And to set this up, Doug
mentioned the 100-mile drive,

00:28:38.390 --> 00:28:40.340
two-hour trip to get down there.

00:28:40.340 --> 00:28:43.640
And having the ability to see
remotely into this vineyard

00:28:43.640 --> 00:28:45.500
was very much a driver.

00:28:45.500 --> 00:28:47.144
And that was the start of it.

00:28:47.144 --> 00:28:48.560
But it really got
its start when I

00:28:48.560 --> 00:28:51.560
watched my wife and her
vineyard consultant sit there

00:28:51.560 --> 00:28:53.330
and auger hole after hole.

00:28:53.330 --> 00:28:54.890
Now, this is a big hand auger.

00:28:54.890 --> 00:28:56.750
It drills down about three feet.

00:28:56.750 --> 00:28:58.460
She pulls it up, and
there's a big clump

00:28:58.460 --> 00:29:00.080
of dirt in the little
cylinder there,

00:29:00.080 --> 00:29:01.496
and they drop it
into their hands,

00:29:01.496 --> 00:29:05.152
and they start squeezing it,
and they yell out, 55%, 60%.

00:29:05.152 --> 00:29:07.610
And I just looked at them and
I said, what the hell are you

00:29:07.610 --> 00:29:08.110
doing?

00:29:08.110 --> 00:29:10.876
And they told me they're
checking for soil moisture.

00:29:10.876 --> 00:29:12.750
And I just decided it
had to be a better way.

00:29:12.750 --> 00:29:15.080
And in my vision as
I thought about this,

00:29:15.080 --> 00:29:17.270
I could see what was
happening up above the ground

00:29:17.270 --> 00:29:18.260
to the plant.

00:29:18.260 --> 00:29:20.210
I wanted to see into the ground.

00:29:20.210 --> 00:29:22.580
What was happening to the
water when it went in?

00:29:22.580 --> 00:29:24.990
And what was happening to
the water when it went out?

00:29:24.990 --> 00:29:30.080
And that's really what
this section's all about

00:29:30.080 --> 00:29:32.640
So why don't you click on--

00:29:32.640 --> 00:29:34.490
just as a general
orientation, the top

00:29:34.490 --> 00:29:37.220
is what we're irrigating,
this little map.

00:29:37.220 --> 00:29:39.920
Right there, the
brown part over here

00:29:39.920 --> 00:29:41.410
is really a map of the vineyard.

00:29:41.410 --> 00:29:42.410
Those are all the zones.

00:29:42.410 --> 00:29:44.690
Oh, what did you do, Doug?

00:29:44.690 --> 00:29:46.715
We just disintegrated
the vineyard.

00:29:46.715 --> 00:29:50.060
DOUG STANDLEY: It just
decided to get diverted again.

00:29:50.060 --> 00:29:52.770
Help!

00:29:52.770 --> 00:29:53.720
Tim will help.

00:29:53.720 --> 00:29:55.470
PHIL ASMUNDSON: But
seeing in the ground's

00:29:55.470 --> 00:29:57.940
important because nobody
really knows what to do.

00:29:57.940 --> 00:30:01.110
You don't know what happens
when it's 101 degrees out.

00:30:01.110 --> 00:30:04.000
And if you ask 10 vineyard
operators down there,

00:30:04.000 --> 00:30:06.510
you'll get 10 totally
different answers

00:30:06.510 --> 00:30:08.994
as to what the right way
to treat your vines are.

00:30:08.994 --> 00:30:10.410
And for the most
part, people just

00:30:10.410 --> 00:30:15.510
said, put 20 gallons of water
on per vine every five days

00:30:15.510 --> 00:30:16.290
and just do that.

00:30:16.290 --> 00:30:18.810
But the fact of the matter
is, soil is all different.

00:30:18.810 --> 00:30:20.226
And you're going
to see that here.

00:30:20.226 --> 00:30:21.579
So there's our map.

00:30:21.579 --> 00:30:22.870
We've got another little chart.

00:30:22.870 --> 00:30:23.940
OK, so you've gone into five.

00:30:23.940 --> 00:30:24.240
That's fine.

00:30:24.240 --> 00:30:25.720
Let's just go
there for a minute.

00:30:25.720 --> 00:30:28.140
So this is where it
all comes together.

00:30:28.140 --> 00:30:29.840
Let's go back to five Doug.

00:30:29.840 --> 00:30:30.870
That's five.

00:30:30.870 --> 00:30:33.960
You'll see at the top that 100.8
degrees, that's that 101 you

00:30:33.960 --> 00:30:35.550
saw before.

00:30:35.550 --> 00:30:39.367
You'll see a recommended
gallons of 11.

00:30:39.367 --> 00:30:40.950
That's actually what
we're putting on.

00:30:40.950 --> 00:30:42.792
If we went back up at
the irrigation screen,

00:30:42.792 --> 00:30:45.000
you'll see that five is
scheduled to be watered today

00:30:45.000 --> 00:30:46.860
for 11 gallons.

00:30:46.860 --> 00:30:49.950
You'll see something called
available water, 54.6%,

00:30:49.950 --> 00:30:52.290
and field capacity, 84%.

00:30:52.290 --> 00:30:53.910
I'll go a little
into field capacity.

00:30:53.910 --> 00:30:56.010
I'll try to stay away from
available water content

00:30:56.010 --> 00:30:58.367
except to just give you
an idea of what it is.

00:30:58.367 --> 00:30:59.700
Why don't you scroll down, Doug.

00:30:59.700 --> 00:31:02.640
And we'll get into the
zone five sensor five area.

00:31:02.640 --> 00:31:08.660
The numbers on the left-hand
side of that chart over here

00:31:08.660 --> 00:31:10.560
and the numbers of
the left are actually

00:31:10.560 --> 00:31:12.750
the current soil
moisture percentages

00:31:12.750 --> 00:31:14.010
at each of those depths.

00:31:14.010 --> 00:31:20.040
Those depths are 4, 8,
16, 24, 32, and 40 inches.

00:31:20.040 --> 00:31:22.860
The number next to that
is the degree of the soil.

00:31:22.860 --> 00:31:25.360
So as you would expect, it
gets cooler as it goes down.

00:31:25.360 --> 00:31:27.060
It's going to be
warmest up at the top

00:31:27.060 --> 00:31:28.620
and get cooler going down.

00:31:28.620 --> 00:31:30.090
Those are more
important to us when

00:31:30.090 --> 00:31:32.400
we're approaching bud break,
when the plant actually

00:31:32.400 --> 00:31:35.760
comes back up and you start
to see green because there's

00:31:35.760 --> 00:31:37.980
certain temperatures for
different varietals, that

00:31:37.980 --> 00:31:39.760
will indicate when
that's about to happen.

00:31:39.760 --> 00:31:42.300
So not quite as important
because remember, this is now.

00:31:42.300 --> 00:31:43.320
This is during veraison.

00:31:43.320 --> 00:31:45.510
And if nobody knows
what veraison is,

00:31:45.510 --> 00:31:48.270
it means when these hard,
green berries start to soften.

00:31:48.270 --> 00:31:50.400
And in the case of red
grapes, they turn red.

00:31:50.400 --> 00:31:52.050
In the case of
white wine grapes,

00:31:52.050 --> 00:31:54.060
they turn golden in color.

00:31:54.060 --> 00:31:57.090
So this is the
start, the prequel

00:31:57.090 --> 00:32:01.260
to the actual ripening process
different during harvest.

00:32:01.260 --> 00:32:03.870
Those buttons in the middle,
the two that are green,

00:32:03.870 --> 00:32:05.940
are green because
right now those

00:32:05.940 --> 00:32:07.320
are our zones of interest.

00:32:07.320 --> 00:32:10.440
We want to try to make pure
water gets to those zones

00:32:10.440 --> 00:32:13.200
because those numbers over
there on the right-hand side

00:32:13.200 --> 00:32:15.780
is the percent of water
that is being taken up

00:32:15.780 --> 00:32:17.387
at each of those depths.

00:32:17.387 --> 00:32:19.220
So let's just think
about that for a moment.

00:32:19.220 --> 00:32:20.678
So what I know, I
said I want to be

00:32:20.678 --> 00:32:22.070
able to look into the ground.

00:32:22.070 --> 00:32:25.150
I not only watch the water as
it goes down into the ground,

00:32:25.150 --> 00:32:27.300
but I watch it as it comes out.

00:32:27.300 --> 00:32:29.280
And if you could just
click on-- why don't you

00:32:29.280 --> 00:32:32.880
click on the 11-gallon
per vine irrigation

00:32:32.880 --> 00:32:35.000
up there on this left
side, Doug, 7-12.

00:32:39.420 --> 00:32:40.390
Oh, that's the 10.

00:32:40.390 --> 00:32:43.120
Go down to 11 if you could.

00:32:43.120 --> 00:32:44.500
He was always a great partner.

00:32:44.500 --> 00:32:45.280
But he did not listen very well.

00:32:45.280 --> 00:32:46.130
DOUG STANDLEY: Where do
you want me at, Phil?

00:32:46.130 --> 00:32:48.855
PHIL ASMUNDSON: The 11
gallon one, go down 7-12.

00:32:48.855 --> 00:32:49.520
There you go.

00:32:49.520 --> 00:32:50.645
DOUG STANDLEY: There we go.

00:32:50.645 --> 00:32:52.210
PHIL ASMUNDSON: So
you got to look up

00:32:52.210 --> 00:32:53.050
a little bit on this one.

00:32:53.050 --> 00:32:55.549
But what you see is this tells
me what the starting and soil

00:32:55.549 --> 00:32:58.570
moisture percentages were,
what the ending ones were.

00:32:58.570 --> 00:33:01.990
And then what this
plus 24 is, this

00:33:01.990 --> 00:33:03.950
is important the way
water dynamics work.

00:33:03.950 --> 00:33:05.560
And believe it or
not, it's incredibly

00:33:05.560 --> 00:33:07.960
consistent from
irrigation to irrigation.

00:33:07.960 --> 00:33:10.030
If you look all the
way over to the left,

00:33:10.030 --> 00:33:13.990
you'll see something called
Observed FC, or field capacity.

00:33:13.990 --> 00:33:16.240
Think of that as
the glass is full.

00:33:16.240 --> 00:33:19.720
That's the point at which
the soil can hold the water

00:33:19.720 --> 00:33:21.460
against the force of gravity.

00:33:21.460 --> 00:33:24.010
And you really find that out
not when the irrigation is

00:33:24.010 --> 00:33:25.630
done because you'll
see those numbers

00:33:25.630 --> 00:33:28.275
are higher than the 24 ones.

00:33:28.275 --> 00:33:29.650
And what they're
doing is they're

00:33:29.650 --> 00:33:31.930
coming back down
to field capacity

00:33:31.930 --> 00:33:33.850
in about 24 hours later.

00:33:33.850 --> 00:33:36.040
It's at that point
that I start capturing

00:33:36.040 --> 00:33:40.210
how much water the vines
are pulling from each depth.

00:33:40.210 --> 00:33:44.260
And if you follow across,
I think it's 66.73 and 67--

00:33:44.260 --> 00:33:45.610
I'll see it better down here--

00:33:45.610 --> 00:33:48.940
and 66 to 66.39.

00:33:48.940 --> 00:33:51.830
That'll almost be the exact
same thing all the time.

00:33:51.830 --> 00:33:53.762
So that's why when
I'm looking at this,

00:33:53.762 --> 00:33:55.720
and I'm trying to figure
out what I want to do,

00:33:55.720 --> 00:33:59.230
I actually went in and looked
at that 11-gallon per vine

00:33:59.230 --> 00:34:00.850
watering to see what happened.

00:34:00.850 --> 00:34:02.710
And that's the
one I chose to do.

00:34:02.710 --> 00:34:07.120
Now, you'll see that if
you went down to 15--

00:34:07.120 --> 00:34:09.460
go down to the 15 on,
7-17 for a moment,

00:34:09.460 --> 00:34:11.590
just to show a difference--

00:34:11.590 --> 00:34:13.400
7-7, sorry.

00:34:13.400 --> 00:34:15.978
Down, there you go, right there.

00:34:15.978 --> 00:34:20.440
You can see we practiced this
a lot, which we did, actually.

00:34:20.440 --> 00:34:24.639
What you'll notice on this
one is the four and eight

00:34:24.639 --> 00:34:27.760
ended at 66.7 and 65.54.

00:34:27.760 --> 00:34:30.909
And they had a little bit
of gain in the 16 inches.

00:34:30.909 --> 00:34:33.739
But we used four
gallons more to do that.

00:34:33.739 --> 00:34:35.830
And the reality is as you
looked at that uptake,

00:34:35.830 --> 00:34:38.686
it's really not taking
water from 16 inches.

00:34:38.686 --> 00:34:40.060
So this is what
we also consider.

00:34:40.060 --> 00:34:42.250
We don't want to over water.

00:34:42.250 --> 00:34:44.570
I think of water as the new oil.

00:34:44.570 --> 00:34:47.230
So we're constantly trying to
minimize the amount of water

00:34:47.230 --> 00:34:49.850
but hit the zones
that we want to hit.

00:34:49.850 --> 00:34:51.400
That's what's most important.

00:34:51.400 --> 00:34:53.230
And we use that
by making sure we

00:34:53.230 --> 00:34:56.194
know what FC is, tracking
each one of these irrigation.

00:34:56.194 --> 00:34:58.360
And that's what we're
building our algorithms around

00:34:58.360 --> 00:34:59.818
so that nio will
just do it for us.

00:34:59.818 --> 00:35:01.120
It already is.

00:35:01.120 --> 00:35:05.410
If you'd gone back to
one of those earlier--

00:35:05.410 --> 00:35:08.620
I think it was actually the
dashboard-- it was recommending

00:35:08.620 --> 00:35:11.530
five for 11.7 gallons.

00:35:11.530 --> 00:35:12.750
I wouldn't go there, Doug.

00:35:12.750 --> 00:35:17.302
But if you can now Doug
click on the open--

00:35:17.302 --> 00:35:18.010
what's it called?

00:35:18.010 --> 00:35:19.720
Yeah, that one, there you go.

00:35:19.720 --> 00:35:20.800
So wait a second here.

00:35:20.800 --> 00:35:21.841
And you'll see something.

00:35:21.841 --> 00:35:24.250
And this is where it
really came to life for us.

00:35:24.250 --> 00:35:27.580
This is a chart for the
last about a month that

00:35:27.580 --> 00:35:30.520
shows the red is irrigations,
and the blue line

00:35:30.520 --> 00:35:31.720
is showing where we went to.

00:35:31.720 --> 00:35:35.500
And you'll notice there's great
symmetry amongst all of these.

00:35:35.500 --> 00:35:37.840
This is exactly what
we're trying to do.

00:35:37.840 --> 00:35:41.200
When you can start to manipulate
the water in the ground

00:35:41.200 --> 00:35:43.060
and know what the
vines are taking up,

00:35:43.060 --> 00:35:46.620
you have suddenly an opportunity
in the early part of the year,

00:35:46.620 --> 00:35:48.280
I'll raise all those lines up.

00:35:48.280 --> 00:35:50.020
I'll give it as much
water as it wants.

00:35:50.020 --> 00:35:50.520
Why?

00:35:50.520 --> 00:35:52.180
Because I'm trying
to grow the canopy.

00:35:52.180 --> 00:35:54.130
I'll do that right
into early May.

00:35:54.130 --> 00:35:57.520
At the end of May when
the fruit is about to set,

00:35:57.520 --> 00:36:00.130
I'll actually bring
those lines down

00:36:00.130 --> 00:36:02.590
because I'm trying to set
very small berries for a very

00:36:02.590 --> 00:36:05.540
simple reason, the
flavor is in the skins.

00:36:05.540 --> 00:36:09.640
So if the berries are
smaller, your fruitful skin

00:36:09.640 --> 00:36:12.910
is a greater ratio to the pulp
of the berry of the juice.

00:36:12.910 --> 00:36:16.240
And so by definition
or theory, you're

00:36:16.240 --> 00:36:17.830
going to have more
flavorful grapes.

00:36:17.830 --> 00:36:19.080
DOUG STANDLEY: Now
every one of you

00:36:19.080 --> 00:36:21.070
are going to be able to go
out, and get drunk, and buy

00:36:21.070 --> 00:36:23.090
a vineyard after this with
a lot more confidence.

00:36:23.090 --> 00:36:25.173
But Philip, we've got to
reel this in a little bit

00:36:25.173 --> 00:36:26.734
and get to the benefits.

00:36:26.734 --> 00:36:27.880
PHIL ASMUNDSON: OK.

00:36:27.880 --> 00:36:28.580
I'll do that.

00:36:28.580 --> 00:36:30.350
DOUG STANDLEY: All right.

00:36:30.350 --> 00:36:31.810
So let me close out this.

00:36:31.810 --> 00:36:32.860
PHIL ASMUNDSON: So you're
going to take me out of that.

00:36:32.860 --> 00:36:36.170
Anyway, suffice it to say, this
is a total game changer for us.

00:36:36.170 --> 00:36:37.850
We have confidence
when we go to bed

00:36:37.850 --> 00:36:39.892
that we know we've watered
what we need to water.

00:36:39.892 --> 00:36:41.974
I don't wake up in the
middle of the night saying,

00:36:41.974 --> 00:36:43.354
oh my God, have we watered this?

00:36:43.354 --> 00:36:44.020
We know we have.

00:36:44.020 --> 00:36:45.820
Nio helps us do that.

00:36:45.820 --> 00:36:48.400
DOUG STANDLEY: OK Phil,
let's get to the meat of it.

00:36:48.400 --> 00:36:49.382
Show me the money!

00:36:49.382 --> 00:36:50.840
PHIL ASMUNDSON:
Show you the money.

00:36:50.840 --> 00:36:52.510
Well, it's pretty
straightforward.

00:36:52.510 --> 00:36:54.550
30% labor efficiency
because we have

00:36:54.550 --> 00:36:57.280
them doing the right
things at the right time.

00:36:57.280 --> 00:36:58.120
We're not guessing.

00:36:58.120 --> 00:37:00.490
We have so much better
information about our vines

00:37:00.490 --> 00:37:03.610
that we can direct our workforce
to do what we need them to do

00:37:03.610 --> 00:37:05.780
when we need them to do that.

00:37:05.780 --> 00:37:07.540
It's says 40%
reduction of water.

00:37:07.540 --> 00:37:10.490
That's my guesstimate
for over the year.

00:37:10.490 --> 00:37:14.800
And in June it was well
over 50% in terms of gallons

00:37:14.800 --> 00:37:19.450
applied in 2018 versus 2017.

00:37:19.450 --> 00:37:21.580
And up to about
a million gallons

00:37:21.580 --> 00:37:23.110
will be saved this
year at Deep Sky.

00:37:23.110 --> 00:37:25.292
And remember, this is a
small, 20-acre vineyard.

00:37:25.292 --> 00:37:27.750
Now Doug, I think you have some
pretty interesting numbers.

00:37:27.750 --> 00:37:30.800
DOUG STANDLEY: Yeah,
so take this notion.

00:37:30.800 --> 00:37:33.490
A 15-acre vineyard
in Willcox, Arizona

00:37:33.490 --> 00:37:37.710
saved a million
gallons of water.

00:37:37.710 --> 00:37:41.580
There 18 and 1/2 billion
acres under plant worldwide

00:37:41.580 --> 00:37:42.510
of viticulture.

00:37:42.510 --> 00:37:46.510
If we got one-seventh of
these results worldwide,

00:37:46.510 --> 00:37:50.680
that's 185 billion gallons
of water that could be saved.

00:37:50.680 --> 00:37:53.380
Now, as I mentioned earlier,
coincidentally, farming

00:37:53.380 --> 00:37:56.450
wastes about 60% of the
water that it's provided.

00:37:56.450 --> 00:37:59.430
Philip is running
50% to 60% savings.

00:37:59.430 --> 00:38:02.220
So the value of
instrumentation--

00:38:02.220 --> 00:38:03.690
think of agriculture
as a metaphor

00:38:03.690 --> 00:38:08.220
to many, many other markets,
and the value of connectivity,

00:38:08.220 --> 00:38:10.960
and frankly, a proper platform.

00:38:10.960 --> 00:38:12.470
So do you want to wrap this up?

00:38:12.470 --> 00:38:13.553
PHIL ASMUNDSON: Yep, sure.

00:38:13.553 --> 00:38:15.840
And those last two, crop
efficiency and price lift,

00:38:15.840 --> 00:38:19.120
I look at those
together a little bit.

00:38:19.120 --> 00:38:20.890
And this is really
all about quality.

00:38:20.890 --> 00:38:22.560
At the end of the
day, everyone wants

00:38:22.560 --> 00:38:23.670
to know how much
water we're saving.

00:38:23.670 --> 00:38:24.810
And we are saving
a lot of water.

00:38:24.810 --> 00:38:26.340
But I would say it's
not about saving water

00:38:26.340 --> 00:38:28.270
as much it's about using
the right amount of water.

00:38:28.270 --> 00:38:30.750
But if you use the right amount,
you will definitely save.

00:38:30.750 --> 00:38:32.340
But it's also about
quality of fruit.

00:38:32.340 --> 00:38:36.030
Quality of fruit definitely
yields higher quality wine.

00:38:36.030 --> 00:38:38.920
We like to say 80% of the
wine is grown in the field.

00:38:38.920 --> 00:38:40.410
And we're actually seeing that.

00:38:40.410 --> 00:38:42.840
And quality is a
subjective thing.

00:38:42.840 --> 00:38:43.749
You can guess at it.

00:38:43.749 --> 00:38:44.540
You can look at it.

00:38:44.540 --> 00:38:45.415
But you can taste it.

00:38:45.415 --> 00:38:47.081
You can get the tannins.

00:38:47.081 --> 00:38:49.080
You can get the ripeness
of the fruit, the fruit

00:38:49.080 --> 00:38:50.220
flavors, and all that.

00:38:50.220 --> 00:38:51.926
But I'd rather look
at my customers

00:38:51.926 --> 00:38:53.550
because we don't keep
all of our fruit.

00:38:53.550 --> 00:38:56.100
We sell a little
less than half of it.

00:38:56.100 --> 00:38:59.610
And we have three main buyers.

00:38:59.610 --> 00:39:03.060
Every one of those buyers now
puts on their label something

00:39:03.060 --> 00:39:04.620
called "Vineyard Designation."

00:39:04.620 --> 00:39:07.860
And their bottles, even though
it will say name of winery,

00:39:07.860 --> 00:39:10.050
it'll say Deep Sky Vineyard
right there on the label

00:39:10.050 --> 00:39:10.916
as well.

00:39:10.916 --> 00:39:12.540
They're doing that
because we've become

00:39:12.540 --> 00:39:14.760
known for higher quality fruit.

00:39:14.760 --> 00:39:17.310
And just putting our
name on their bottles

00:39:17.310 --> 00:39:22.080
lifts their ability to sell it
for a greater amount of money.

00:39:22.080 --> 00:39:25.230
To me, that's the real kicker
to me because it really is--

00:39:25.230 --> 00:39:27.270
our customers are
telling us your quality

00:39:27.270 --> 00:39:30.014
is better, which has allowed
us to lift our prices.

00:39:30.014 --> 00:39:30.930
None of them are here.

00:39:30.930 --> 00:39:32.520
But it's going up
next year as well

00:39:32.520 --> 00:39:35.520
because we're really excited
about our crop this year.

00:39:35.520 --> 00:39:38.760
One last thing to look at,
75% lower cost of human error.

00:39:38.760 --> 00:39:39.630
That was real.

00:39:39.630 --> 00:39:41.160
We had people not showing up.

00:39:41.160 --> 00:39:42.490
We live in Marana, Arizona.

00:39:42.490 --> 00:39:44.190
This is in Willcox, Arizona.

00:39:44.190 --> 00:39:46.319
We had one month
where the guy told us,

00:39:46.319 --> 00:39:47.860
oh yeah, water, and
water, and water.

00:39:47.860 --> 00:39:51.060
And we got our electric
bill, it was about $20.

00:39:51.060 --> 00:39:53.970
You couldn't run that pump
for three or four hours

00:39:53.970 --> 00:39:55.867
without it being 20 bucks.

00:39:55.867 --> 00:39:57.200
And the guy just hadn't done it.

00:39:57.200 --> 00:40:00.030
And we couldn't be
there all the time,

00:40:00.030 --> 00:40:01.620
but we needed to
have visibility.

00:40:01.620 --> 00:40:03.210
We took a cruise in
Alaska last year.

00:40:03.210 --> 00:40:05.160
We were we were
monitoring that vineyard

00:40:05.160 --> 00:40:07.380
during the entire
cruise, putting on water,

00:40:07.380 --> 00:40:09.270
looking at needs of
water, all of that,

00:40:09.270 --> 00:40:12.660
all from a ship somewhere
in a fjord in Alaska.

00:40:12.660 --> 00:40:14.160
With that Doug,
turn it over to you.

00:40:14.160 --> 00:40:16.451
DOUG STANDLEY: So the road
map and our progress to date

00:40:16.451 --> 00:40:20.400
is, one tip is, again, if you
avoid the overthinking, the

00:40:20.400 --> 00:40:22.800
how, but look for
a really quick win.

00:40:22.800 --> 00:40:24.780
Now in this case,
the quick win was

00:40:24.780 --> 00:40:27.240
having Phillip stop calling
me screaming about his wife

00:40:27.240 --> 00:40:29.910
having to drive two hours to
check to see if the water was

00:40:29.910 --> 00:40:33.900
on or not trusting the people
managing their vineyard.

00:40:33.900 --> 00:40:36.930
So we got them the
confidence and the quick

00:40:36.930 --> 00:40:40.090
win that they could remotely
manage his vineyard very,

00:40:40.090 --> 00:40:41.094
very quickly.

00:40:41.094 --> 00:40:42.510
The other thing
that we discovered

00:40:42.510 --> 00:40:44.940
is that their entire
thesis about water usage

00:40:44.940 --> 00:40:46.440
frankly was wrong.

00:40:46.440 --> 00:40:48.402
They thought they
were distributing--

00:40:48.402 --> 00:40:50.610
I forget how many gallons
it was now-- but literally,

00:40:50.610 --> 00:40:53.740
it was one-fifth was
the actual amount.

00:40:53.740 --> 00:40:55.166
Once we instrumented
the vineyard,

00:40:55.166 --> 00:40:56.790
the water that was
getting to the plant

00:40:56.790 --> 00:40:59.790
was a small percentage of
what they thought it was.

00:40:59.790 --> 00:41:02.070
So that was a real quick win.

00:41:02.070 --> 00:41:05.280
The first year we
connected the system,

00:41:05.280 --> 00:41:09.150
got it stabilized, really
focused on the user experience,

00:41:09.150 --> 00:41:11.160
made some adjustments to it.

00:41:11.160 --> 00:41:13.740
Last year, we made
further modifications.

00:41:13.740 --> 00:41:15.180
Went in and tuned
some algorithms.

00:41:15.180 --> 00:41:19.170
Phil does as a lot of
back work, data crunching.

00:41:19.170 --> 00:41:21.160
This year has been
extremely productive.

00:41:21.160 --> 00:41:23.820
We're turning the ROI up.

00:41:23.820 --> 00:41:25.112
He's transforming his business.

00:41:25.112 --> 00:41:26.611
As he mentioned,
he's getting things

00:41:26.611 --> 00:41:27.780
like vineyard designates.

00:41:27.780 --> 00:41:29.340
So we're really
achieving the goals.

00:41:29.340 --> 00:41:31.929
Frankly, none of
this is a surprise

00:41:31.929 --> 00:41:32.970
from the original vision.

00:41:32.970 --> 00:41:35.200
We had alignment that
this could happen.

00:41:35.200 --> 00:41:37.020
But frankly, there's
a leap of faith.

00:41:37.020 --> 00:41:38.520
So we focused hard
on the quick win.

00:41:38.520 --> 00:41:40.710
And now we're really
seeing-- no pun intended--

00:41:40.710 --> 00:41:42.420
the fruit of our effort.

00:41:42.420 --> 00:41:44.190
Now, the super exciting
part, particularly

00:41:44.190 --> 00:41:45.564
with the partnership
with Google,

00:41:45.564 --> 00:41:47.760
is we're really looking
forward to next year,

00:41:47.760 --> 00:41:54.210
refining this instrumentation
and computation model,

00:41:54.210 --> 00:41:59.010
starting to incorporate ML both
from a data standpoint as well

00:41:59.010 --> 00:42:00.150
as some video analytics.

00:42:00.150 --> 00:42:01.840
We can talk more
about that offline.

00:42:01.840 --> 00:42:03.700
We're getting short on time.

00:42:03.700 --> 00:42:07.660
But frankly, this
project is now--

00:42:07.660 --> 00:42:09.420
it's just too compelling.

00:42:09.420 --> 00:42:13.080
We as a company are thinking
about how do we scale this.

00:42:13.080 --> 00:42:15.330
So even though we're
going to stay horizontal,

00:42:15.330 --> 00:42:16.700
we're now very focused on this.

00:42:16.700 --> 00:42:21.330
The results are just undeniable.

00:42:21.330 --> 00:42:24.750
Lastly for the tech audience,
we'll give you a quick flyby.

00:42:24.750 --> 00:42:26.745
I think now this is
the second day of Next,

00:42:26.745 --> 00:42:30.240
so you've seen slides that
looks something like this.

00:42:30.240 --> 00:42:31.980
But this is really
an elegant marriage

00:42:31.980 --> 00:42:34.470
between Google
handling the cloud

00:42:34.470 --> 00:42:37.620
elements and our nio platform
handling everything below.

00:42:37.620 --> 00:42:41.250
We're a distributed edge
processing platform.

00:42:41.250 --> 00:42:43.140
So you see the data flow.

00:42:43.140 --> 00:42:46.630
It my world, it starts
in the far right.

00:42:46.630 --> 00:42:50.460
We have tightly wrapped IoT
core into our deployment model.

00:42:50.460 --> 00:42:51.030
We use it.

00:42:51.030 --> 00:42:52.260
It's functioning.

00:42:52.260 --> 00:42:54.960
We use it in
everything we do now.

00:42:54.960 --> 00:42:56.550
It's working beautifully.

00:42:56.550 --> 00:42:59.100
Google's involving us in their
forward thinking about where

00:42:59.100 --> 00:43:00.690
the product line goes.

00:43:00.690 --> 00:43:02.800
And we're super
excited about that.

00:43:02.800 --> 00:43:05.880
But remember now, this
is Willcox, Arizona.

00:43:05.880 --> 00:43:08.340
Adam joked about, if you're
not at the bottom of the ocean.

00:43:08.340 --> 00:43:10.405
But Willcox, Arizona
is about like being

00:43:10.405 --> 00:43:11.530
in the bottom of the ocean.

00:43:11.530 --> 00:43:14.110
It is a very, very remote place.

00:43:14.110 --> 00:43:17.610
Our gross network pipe is
a 10.5 rural cell card.

00:43:17.610 --> 00:43:18.490
That's it.

00:43:18.490 --> 00:43:19.572
That's all we've got.

00:43:19.572 --> 00:43:21.780
And we're very, very efficient
in network management.

00:43:21.780 --> 00:43:24.226
We have numerous low-power
networks operating here.

00:43:24.226 --> 00:43:25.350
But there's a lot going on.

00:43:25.350 --> 00:43:28.620
So we run a local
network at the vineyard.

00:43:28.620 --> 00:43:29.730
The vineyard's got to run.

00:43:29.730 --> 00:43:31.980
It can't be dependent on
internet connectivity.

00:43:31.980 --> 00:43:34.500
And then, of course, we
then filter and share

00:43:34.500 --> 00:43:38.730
up to the cloud with Pub/Sub,
up through into Dataflow,

00:43:38.730 --> 00:43:39.660
BigQuery.

00:43:39.660 --> 00:43:41.850
And think of it as our
data warehouse there.

00:43:41.850 --> 00:43:44.100
And of course, everything
is deployed with Kubernetes.

00:43:44.100 --> 00:43:48.720
So numerous nodes
ranging from quad core

00:43:48.720 --> 00:43:51.690
down to single-core
nodes because nio

00:43:51.690 --> 00:43:53.880
is not just a
data-capturing platform.

00:43:53.880 --> 00:43:56.020
This is a full control system.

00:43:56.020 --> 00:43:58.890
This vineyard runs on auto mode.

00:43:58.890 --> 00:44:00.660
it does not have to--

00:44:00.660 --> 00:44:02.665
literally, nio will
make recommendations

00:44:02.665 --> 00:44:03.540
and run the vineyard.

00:44:03.540 --> 00:44:05.211
So we've got mobile tools.

00:44:05.211 --> 00:44:06.460
We have a lots going on there.

00:44:06.460 --> 00:44:09.730
We have recently introduced
a little bit of LoRa as well.

00:44:09.730 --> 00:44:11.680
So that's super exciting.

00:44:11.680 --> 00:44:14.370
But for me, it's just it's
a magnificent, elegant

00:44:14.370 --> 00:44:18.010
collaboration between Google,
and us, and a fantastic

00:44:18.010 --> 00:44:18.510
customer.

00:44:18.510 --> 00:44:21.114
So it's just a win.

00:44:21.114 --> 00:44:23.280
And on that, I think I'll
turn it back over to Adam.

00:44:23.280 --> 00:44:24.633
ADAM MICHELSON: Sure, thanks.

00:44:24.633 --> 00:44:25.535
Thanks Doug.

00:44:25.535 --> 00:44:27.430
Thanks Phil.

00:44:27.430 --> 00:44:28.430
Is this thing back on?

00:44:28.430 --> 00:44:29.150
Yes.

00:44:29.150 --> 00:44:31.130
So quick closing thoughts.

00:44:31.130 --> 00:44:35.825
So to circle back to the ROI,
I hope that from the context--

00:44:35.825 --> 00:44:39.560
so agriculture is a
major use case for IoT.

00:44:39.560 --> 00:44:41.310
Manufacturing is
a major use case.

00:44:41.310 --> 00:44:45.230
Smart home, medical, we're
seeing a number of verticals

00:44:45.230 --> 00:44:48.460
begin to really
realize the benefits.

00:44:48.460 --> 00:44:49.940
And it often starts in this way.

00:44:49.940 --> 00:44:52.920
It's like, ugh, I don't want
to get up and drive two hours,

00:44:52.920 --> 00:44:55.730
so in these boutique
way are disruptors.

00:44:55.730 --> 00:44:57.480
It's what the big folks
are worried about.

00:44:57.480 --> 00:45:01.140
But for the folks in the room
who are already big folks,

00:45:01.140 --> 00:45:04.394
it's good not to start with
these huge, huge aspirations

00:45:04.394 --> 00:45:05.060
with other data.

00:45:05.060 --> 00:45:06.770
But start with something small.

00:45:06.770 --> 00:45:08.990
Don't drive two hours,
make the CEO happy,

00:45:08.990 --> 00:45:10.770
and then go from there.

00:45:10.770 --> 00:45:13.760
But when looking for
ROI, the good mindset

00:45:13.760 --> 00:45:15.890
is first thing
outside your company.

00:45:15.890 --> 00:45:17.339
What's the macroeconomic trends?

00:45:17.339 --> 00:45:18.380
In this case, it's water.

00:45:18.380 --> 00:45:19.220
It's labor shortage.

00:45:19.220 --> 00:45:20.720
What's happening
in the world that's

00:45:20.720 --> 00:45:24.290
going to affect because we're
skating to the puck with IoT.

00:45:24.290 --> 00:45:26.900
It's going to be a two
to a five-year journey,

00:45:26.900 --> 00:45:28.490
so what's going to happen?

00:45:28.490 --> 00:45:32.180
Competitive disruption, like
we were saying, x as a service.

00:45:32.180 --> 00:45:33.860
So are your
competitors shifting?

00:45:33.860 --> 00:45:35.840
Especially if you're
a manufacturer,

00:45:35.840 --> 00:45:38.480
are they getting into the-- are
they becoming software vendors?

00:45:38.480 --> 00:45:41.600
The minute you launch an app,
you're a software vendor.

00:45:41.600 --> 00:45:43.980
And what's happening
there in your industry?

00:45:43.980 --> 00:45:46.250
And then regulatory, we
didn't talk about here.

00:45:46.250 --> 00:45:48.500
But we see a lot of
regulatory pressure.

00:45:48.500 --> 00:45:51.770
And where that comes in,
IoT is a great release valve

00:45:51.770 --> 00:45:53.960
because IoT provides
a lot of transparency.

00:45:53.960 --> 00:45:57.170
So rather than having
auditors, having expenses

00:45:57.170 --> 00:46:00.930
to deal with regulation,
IoT can deal with it.

00:46:00.930 --> 00:46:02.600
So just some
macroeconomic trends,

00:46:02.600 --> 00:46:04.460
I'm sure you can think of more.

00:46:04.460 --> 00:46:06.590
After that, you think
of your internals

00:46:06.590 --> 00:46:08.300
like the chart we
went through before.

00:46:08.300 --> 00:46:11.780
Internally-focused, where
can we find revenue, get

00:46:11.780 --> 00:46:14.030
as many tick marks as you can?

00:46:14.030 --> 00:46:17.480
Direct revenue first,
indirect second, and see

00:46:17.480 --> 00:46:19.420
if your thesis is achieved.

00:46:19.420 --> 00:46:22.880
It can be achieved in a
very small way at first.

00:46:22.880 --> 00:46:25.160
And lastly, no ROI, no pilot.

00:46:25.160 --> 00:46:28.910
But basically, if you see
80% of your pilot effort--

00:46:28.910 --> 00:46:30.740
and our pilot
effort is precious.

00:46:30.740 --> 00:46:32.930
You all in the
audience can be doing

00:46:32.930 --> 00:46:35.120
lots of things with your time.

00:46:35.120 --> 00:46:38.060
The fact that so many hands
got raised around efforts

00:46:38.060 --> 00:46:43.640
around IoT, we are all
responsible for exiting

00:46:43.640 --> 00:46:46.680
pilot purgatory together
and finding this ROI.

00:46:46.680 --> 00:46:48.999
It's not "Mission Impossible."

00:46:48.999 --> 00:46:50.040
That's a movie reference.

00:46:50.040 --> 00:46:52.550
PHIL ASMUNDSON: Well,
and to that point, Adam,

00:46:52.550 --> 00:46:54.200
we talked about this.

00:46:54.200 --> 00:46:58.370
And if a little vineyard
in Arizona of 20 acres

00:46:58.370 --> 00:47:01.800
can get the kind of
results we've achieved--

00:47:01.800 --> 00:47:03.000
and it wasn't easy.

00:47:03.000 --> 00:47:04.380
There were some fits and starts.

00:47:04.380 --> 00:47:07.480
But if we can do it, I can
only imagine the impact

00:47:07.480 --> 00:47:09.230
you guys can make on
your companies, which

00:47:09.230 --> 00:47:12.620
are much larger and probably
have a much bigger apple

00:47:12.620 --> 00:47:14.981
to take a bite out of the stuff.

00:47:14.981 --> 00:47:16.040
ADAM MICHELSON: And so--

00:47:16.040 --> 00:47:16.540
thanks.

00:47:16.540 --> 00:47:18.680
And so quick thoughts
in terms of wrap up.

00:47:18.680 --> 00:47:20.870
Obviously, we're in the
middle of Next here,

00:47:20.870 --> 00:47:23.330
so we really
appreciate your time.

00:47:23.330 --> 00:47:25.670
Given the number of hands
that went up around IoT,

00:47:25.670 --> 00:47:26.690
we're really excited.

00:47:26.690 --> 00:47:30.750
If you haven't seen
Google's IoT offering yet,

00:47:30.750 --> 00:47:32.150
